		<profileDesc>
			<abstract>
				<p>Recent studies on interpreting the hidden states of speech models have shown their ability to capture speaker-specific features, including gender. Does this finding also hold for speech translation (ST) models? If so, what are the implications for the speaker&apos;s gender assignment in translation? We address these questions from an interpretability perspective, using probing methods to assess gender encoding across diverse ST models. Results on three language directions (English → French/Italian/Spanish) indicate that while traditional encoder-decoder models capture gender information, newer architecturesintegrating a speech encoder with a machine translation system via adapters-do not. We also demonstrate that low gender encoding capabilities result in systems&apos; tendency toward a masculine default, a translation bias that is more pronounced in newer architectures. 1</p>
			</abstract>
